"""Claim-preserving language revision using Academic Writing Humanizer guidance."""
from common import ROOT
import json
R=ROOT/'revision/review_v5';p=R/'manuscript.md';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8');retained=[]
def replace_paragraph(start,new,keep=True):
 global s
 a=s.index(start);b=s.index('\n\n',a);old=s[a:b]
 if keep:retained.append(old)
 s=s[:a]+new+s[b:]
replace_paragraph('The ECMWF S2S AI Challenge archive provides', 'The public ECMWF S2S AI Challenge archive contains 53 Thursday production dates in 2020, each with 20 corresponding hindcast dates during 2000–2019 and 11 members (ECMWF 2021). This provides 1060 origins per domain, using the configurations associated with those production dates. These are retrospective hindcasts rather than forecasts issued in the verification years. Vitart et al. (2017) describe the broader S2S database.')
replace_paragraph('Two archive issues require quality control.', 'Two archive issues require correction. Comparison with original GRIB confirms that a NetCDF missing-value code also represents the lowest rainfall bin, which is restored using each file\'s packing parameters. The audit covers the control member, first hindcast year and four endpoints in all 53 files (Supplementary Note 5).')
replace_paragraph('The second-system check uses public ACCESS-S2', 'The second-system check uses public ACCESS-S2 research hindcasts from NCI (Wedd et al. 2022; Australian Bureau of Meteorology, undated a): nine members, monthly first-day origins during 2000–2018 and the first 28 daily rainfall fields. All eight domains have 228 origins. Fluxes are converted to daily millimetres and interpreted as UTC calendar-day means from their midpoint timestamps. Exact cell overlaps provide domain averages. Source metadata, including the missing time-bounds variable, are retained. Different grids, members and origins require comparisons within each system rather than rankings of their absolute scores.')
replace_paragraph('A separate test uses all 53 Thursday', 'The new-period test uses all 53 Thursday ECMWF operational origins in 2020 and members 0–10, selected in advance to match the 11-member hindcast size. Training includes only origins and targets ending by 31 December 2019. AGCD through 28 January 2021 completes verification. Forecasts were saved before scoring, and control-member GRIB audits cover all four endpoints in every file. This is retrospective transfer to unused issued forecasts, with pooled 2020 hindcast configurations and revised index archives; it does not reconstruct the exact historical operational information set.')
replace_paragraph('The additional predictors are the two real-time', 'Climate predictors comprise RMM1 and RMM2 for the MJO (Wheeler and Hendon 2004), Niño3.4 sea surface temperature, DMI and the station-based SAM index (Marshall 2003). Sources are the Bureau of Meteorology, NOAA and the British Antarctic Survey. RMM is lagged two days; monthly indices use the month two months before initialisation, allowing for reporting delays. Supplementary Note 5 records the sources and timing conventions.')
replace_paragraph('All five predictors are standardised', 'Predictors are standardised within each permitted fitting sample. Niño3.4 uses sea surface temperature, not anomalies estimated from verification observations. Archived revisions make this a retrospective lagged-information experiment. The same predictors and lags apply across domains; post hoc single-index results do not change that set.')
replace_paragraph('Monthly NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis 1 winds', 'Independent monthly NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis 1 wind and humidity fields at 850 hPa, obtained through IRI, describe 2000–2019 circulation at 2.5° resolution (Kalnay et al. 1996). We map calendar-month identities correctly from the source time coordinates. A fixed 120–140°E, 15–5°S zonal-wind average measures the broad monsoon flow; seasonal composites cover 100–155°E, 25°S–5°N. These diagnostics interpret the northern forecast setting and do not enter model fitting.')
replace_paragraph('Each year from 2000 to 2019 is held out', 'Outer validation holds out each year and excludes it and its immediate neighbours from fitting, also removing origins whose targets end in an excluded year. ECMWF folds contain 897–954 training and 53 validation origins; ACCESS-S2 folds verify twelve monthly origins. Permitted years may precede or follow the held-out year. The embargo removes adjacent years and overlapping targets, while more distant climate dependence can remain.')
replace_paragraph('The homogeneous benchmark was specified', 'The homogeneous benchmark precedes the factorial extension, geographical checks and added diagnostics. Dated plans record their sequence and the results already inspected. Figure 2 shows the hierarchy and nested validation; supplementary experiments do not replace the original primary comparisons.')
replace_paragraph('For each origin and marginal distribution, 200', 'Each marginal supplies 200 equally spaced midpoint quantiles. A Schaake shuffle assigns their ranks from observed rainfall trajectories sampled with replacement within 45 calendar days of the origin and within permitted training data (Clark et al. 2004). H0 and H1 share quantile positions, template cases and tie-breaking rules, including identical weekly samples before reconciliation.')
replace_paragraph('The homogeneous benchmark includes ensemble copula', 'A matched dependence benchmark uses ECC ranks from the 11 raw members and an 11-sample historical shuffle (Schefzik et al. 2013). Identical marginal quantiles isolate dependence effects after summation or reconciliation (Supplementary Table S2).')
replace_paragraph('The seven-node summing matrix is','The seven-node summing matrix is',False)
replace_paragraph('The six nonnegative parameters minimise', 'Six nonnegative parameters minimise closed-form CRPS, a distributional forecast loss in rainfall units. Monthly fits use training origins within 45 calendar days of the month\'s fifteenth day, wrapping across the year boundary. Rainfall is scaled by its training mean for optimisation and returned to millimetres. Comparators include simpler seasonal EMOS, training climatology, member-wise quantile mapping and ECMWF (QC); implementation details are in Supplementary Note 5.')
replace_paragraph('The vector z contains the five standardised', 'The vector z contains the five standardised indices. H0 variance and shift remain fixed, so zero coefficients recover the base distribution. Coefficients start at zero, are bounded to [−1, 1], and minimise CRPS divided by training mean rainfall plus 0.05 times their squared norm. The same regularisation applies everywhere.')
replace_paragraph('Lower values are better. The corresponding skill score', 'Lower CRPS is better. CRPSS is one minus mean CRPS divided by the score of permitted seasonal training climatology, evaluated on its full empirical support. Fair CRPS is reported for unequal ensemble sizes (Ferro 2014), alongside matched-size dependence controls because the deterministic quantiles are not independent random draws.')
replace_paragraph('This is equivalent to integrating the CRPS loss', 'This integrates CRPS loss above q. Further diagnostics include exceedance Brier skill relative to training probabilities, interval coverage and width, and randomised PIT histograms. The Hersbach (2000) decomposition separates calibration error, termed reliability, from potential CRPS, which is uncertainty minus resolution. It describes statistical performance rather than a physical mechanism. Joint energy and variogram scores are reported in Supplementary Table S4.')
replace_paragraph('Uncertainty intervals use 10000 bootstrap', 'Primary intervals resample 20 complete origin years 10000 times, with common draws across domains. Two-sided annual sign flips use 100000 draws and Holm adjustment over the three primary contrasts. Descriptive intervals and adjusted tests answer different questions; an interval excluding zero need not imply adjusted rejection. Domain and seasonal contrasts retain descriptive intervals. Details and the earlier homogeneous family are in Supplementary Note 5.')
replace_paragraph('Ωₛ is the seasonal covariance', 'Ωₛ is the seasonal inner-error covariance and its bottom block contains weekly errors. We compare predicted gain with one minus the held-out MSE ratio, reporting bias, slope and Spearman association across 16 domain-season cases. Intervals resample common years. The prediction assumes negligible bias and stable error relationships, which are themselves evaluated.')
replace_paragraph('One interpretation is that rainfall-only marginals', 'Rainfall-only marginals may constrain uncertain climate adjustments. Forecasts at different scales can have complementary errors; adjusting every node could reduce that complementarity or spread a common calibration error. The experiment establishes the score pattern without isolating either mechanism. The small effect of fortnightly nodes further shows that additional aggregation equations do not automatically add useful predictive information.',False)
replace_paragraph('Dependence reconstruction also matters.', 'Dependence reconstruction changes absolute calibration, although matched ECC retains the local H1-minus-H0 direction. The power-map audit also shows why fitting and application populations must match. A separate mixture experiment examines the marginal value of complexity, defined as the additional held-out skill obtained by allowing weights to respond to forecast state. The tested adaptive weights do not consistently improve on fixed weights (Supplementary Note 4); this result concerns that specification rather than adaptive forecasting in general.',False)
# Avoid an editorial comment in the scientific conclusion.
s=s.replace('The result corrects an overly broad conclusion of no reconciliation benefit. It also requires careful attribution:', 'Its interpretation depends on the comparator:')
s=s.replace('The independent reanalysis identifies a clear seasonal reversal','The reanalysis identifies a clear seasonal reversal')
s=s.replace('The independent reanalysis','The reanalysis')
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
(R/'implementation_details.md').write_text('## Supplementary Note 5 Data handling and scoring details\n\n'+'\n\n'.join(retained),encoding='utf-8')
audit=json.loads((R/'editorial_audit.json').read_text());audit['body_words_before_references']=len(s.split('## 1 Introduction')[1].split('## Data availability')[0].split());audit['detail_paragraphs_moved']=len(retained)
(R/'editorial_audit.json').write_text(json.dumps(audit,indent=2));print(audit)
